sors—such as homonyms—used to illustrate linguistic devices in discussions of difference and identity. In the referenced text, RAYMOND ROUSSEL is mentioned alongside Peguy, with no additional attributes provided about this pairing in the given descriptions.", "CHARLES PEGUY:Charles Peguy is a French poet and essayist described here as a great repeater of literature; his technique substitutes repetition by contiguity (synonymity) rather than homonymity, forming a before-language and an auroral language.", "ZARATHUSTRA:Zarathustra (ZARATHUSTRA) is the central figure in Friedrich Nietzsche’s Thus Spoke Zarathustra, a mythical/philosophical prophet and teacher who undergoes crises, convalescence, and metamorphosis as he engages with themes of becoming, self-overcoming, and the drama of time. He functions as a vehicle for Nietzsche’s critique of conventional values and as a vehicle for articulating the idea of the eternal return (eternal recurrence), illustrating how experiences and actions might recur in an endless cycle. In the narrative, Zarathustra is invoked as the prophetic figure who reminds the “buffoon” of human folly and moral complacency, and through his experiences he exemplifies how signs and repetition govern thought and action. He “receives his sign either from too near or from too far,” and only at the end does he discern the correct distance that can turn what is ill in the eternal return into liberatory and redemptive repetition. This makes Zarathustra a dark precursor to the concept of the eternal return, foreshadowing or initiating the idea that life’s patterns can be transformed into meaningful repetition rather than despair. Thus, Zarathustra serves as both a narrative protagonist and a philosophical emblem, embodying the possibility of transformation through crisis, time, and the perpetual revaluation of values—an embodiment of Nietzsche’s vision of becoming and the path toward self-overcoming."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}},